<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Expo 58</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1958 Brussels World's Fair</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Expo 58 was the world's fair held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> from April to October 1958, the first major international exposition after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its theme was a more humane world built on scientific progress, and it drew more than 40 million visits. Its centrepiece, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomium">Atomium</a>, still stands on the Heysel plateau in the north of the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/expo-58.jpg" alt="Expo 58" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harry Pot for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haarlemse_bloemenmeisjes_brengen_bezoek_aan_de_Expo,_Bestanddeelnr_909-5043.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Expo 58 was the eleventh world's fair hosted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the fifth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, after those of 1888, 1897, 1910 and 1935. Belgium won the bid in 1953 over other European capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and the government of Prime Minister Achille Van Acker gave up the national celebrations due in 1955 to put more money into the fair. Nearly 15,000 workers spent three years building the site on the Heysel Plateau, reusing many buildings of the 1935 fair. The theme, Bilan du monde, pour un monde plus humain, drew on faith in technical and scientific progress and on post-war debates over the ethical use of atomic power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>More than forty nations took part, with over forty-five national pavilions besides those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the Belgian Congo. King Baudouin opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomium">Atomium</a>, a giant model of an iron crystal's unit cell, with a call for world peace and social and economic progress. In the Philips Pavilion the Poème électronique was played from 425 loudspeakers placed as designed by Iannis Xenakis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a>. The Czechoslovak pavilion, visited by 6 million people, was named the best of the fair, the Soviet pavilion showed Sputnik prototypes and a model of the nuclear-powered icebreaker Lenin, and the American one showed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a>, a 360° film by Walt Disney Productions.</p><p>The Belgian Congo section was meant above all to display the civilising work claimed for Belgian colonialism, and its village indigène was recognised by the Congolese as a human zoo. The Ministry of Colonies exhibited 598 Congolese, educated city dwellers who were made to wear primitive clothing and were kept by an armed guard from speaking to the visitors. An eight-month-old baby, Juste Bonaventure Langa, died during the exhibit. In mid-July the Congolese protested at their treatment and demanded to be sent home, which ended the exhibit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The fair ran from 17 April to 19 October 1958. It was tied to major redevelopment works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, whose boulevards were turned into urban motorways; a new terminal was added to the Melsbroek national airport, on land of the municipality of Zaventem, and the tram lines built to serve the site from Brussels remained in service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomium">Atomium</a>, meant to last only the six months of the fair, was never taken down. During the fair someone tore a corner from a page of the autograph of Mozart's Requiem, which was on display.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Expo 58” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
